# Cascade — The pointer and the controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: what a press picks up, how a
held run follows and lands, what separates a click from a drop, the double click,
the controls each screen carries, and how the menus over those controls are
driven. The piles these gestures act on are in `specs/table.md`, and the rules
that accept or refuse a move are in `specs/foundations.md`, `specs/tableau.md`,
and `specs/stock.md`.

## How input reaches the game

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the pointer belongs to the runtime layer you
write. What the game reads from that layer is a pointer position in the game's
logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, and
a release edge, one entry per event the page delivered, in the order they
arrived.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The pointer belongs to the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents it in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it
is the samples delivered since the previous frame, each carrying a pointer
position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`)
stage and its press or release edge, in the order they arrived.
{{else}}
The pointer belongs to the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents it in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it
is the samples the player controller is handed each frame, each carrying a
pointer position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`) stage and its press or release edge, in the order they arrived.
{{/if}}

Every sample a frame delivers is answered on its own, in the order it arrived, so
a press and the release that followed it inside one frame both take effect and a
gesture is never reduced to the last position of the frame that carried it.

A mouse and a touchscreen stand on the same footing. A press is a mouse button
going down or a finger touching down, a move is either one travelling, and a
release is the button coming up or the finger lifting. Every control the game
carries answers those three, so the game is complete on a touch device with no
mouse attached, and dragging a card neither scrolls nor zooms the page.

## What a press picks up

The rules of this section are the `playing` screen's. On `title` and `howto` the
pointer answers that screen's controls, as Menu navigation below states, and on
`won` a press deals a fresh game, as `specs/victory.md` states.

A press resolves to the card drawn over every other card at the press point,
which in a column is the lowest of the cards whose footprint contains that point.
What it lifts depends on that card:

| The card under the press | What the press lifts |
| --- | --- |
| A face-up card in a column | That card and every card below it in the column, as the run `specs/tableau.md` defines |
| The waste's top card | That card alone |
| A foundation's top card | That card alone |
| A face-down card | Nothing |
| A card on the waste that is not its top card | Nothing |

A press that lands on no card lifts nothing, and so does a press on the stock.

The run enters the hand on the press itself, before the pointer has moved at all,
and it is the run in hand from that moment until the gesture ends. It leaves the
pile it was lifted from as it enters the hand, so that pile holds only the cards
left behind for as long as the gesture lasts. The run holds the position the
pointer gives it: it keeps the offset between the press point and the leading
card's top-left, so the run travels exactly as far as the pointer does, and it is
drawn above the piles it passes over.

While a run is held, the pile that would accept it, if any, is the drop target,
and it is drawn as highlighted. A pile is the drop target only while the release
rule below would resolve the run to it and that pile accepts the run. The target
is recomputed as the pointer moves.

## A click and a drop

A gesture runs from a press to the release that follows it. The release decides
which of the two kinds of gesture it was:

| The release point | The gesture |
| --- | --- |
| Within `DRAG_THRESHOLD` (`5`) of the press point | A click |
| Farther than `DRAG_THRESHOLD` from the press point | A drop |

A click does both of the following, in this order:

1. It returns any held run to the pile it was lifted from, in the order it was
   lifted, with every face unchanged.
2. It turns the stock, as `specs/stock.md` states, when the press point lies in
   the stock's drop rectangle.

A drop resolves the run in hand against the drop rectangles `specs/table.md`
fixes. It turns no stock.

Either kind of gesture activates a control when one control's hit region holds
both the press point and the release point, as Menu navigation below states.

A release with nothing in hand that activates no control and turns no stock
changes nothing.

## Where a drop lands

A drop resolves to the pile whose drop rectangle contains the **center of the
run's leading card**, that card being the one drawn at the top of the held run.
The rectangles do not overlap, so that center lies in at most one of them.

| Where the leading card's center lies | The result |
| --- | --- |
| In the rectangle of a pile that accepts the run | The move is applied: the run leaves its source and lands on that pile |
| In the rectangle of a pile that refuses the run | The run returns to the pile it was lifted from |
| In no pile's rectangle | The run returns to the pile it was lifted from |

An applied move is a move like any other, so a column it empties of face-up cards
turns its newly exposed card, as `specs/tableau.md` states, and a board it
completes wins the game, as `specs/victory.md` states.

## The double click

A press is a double click when all three hold:

- it arrives within `DOUBLE_CLICK_WINDOW` (`0.30`) seconds of game time of the
  previous press;
- its point lies within `DOUBLE_CLICK_SLOP` (`20`) of the previous press's point;
- it lands on a playable card.

A playable card is the waste's top card or a column's lowest face-up card.

A double click lifts nothing. Instead the card under it is sent to the foundation
it belongs on, when that foundation accepts it by `specs/foundations.md`, and the
gesture ends there; the release that follows changes nothing. A card the
foundations refuse stays where it is. Sending a card home this way is a move like
any other, so it turns a newly exposed column card and it can win the game.

Every press records its point and the game time it arrived at, and that is the
press the next one is measured against.

## The controls

Each control occupies a rectangular hit region the build lays out, and its label
is drawn inside that region. `specs/instrumentation.md` states the reading
through which the build reports an item's region in logical units, so the
controls keep the layout the build chose. What each one does is in
`specs/screens.md`.

| Screen | Its controls, in order |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The two items of `TITLE_ITEMS` |
| `howto` | The one item labelled `HOWTO_BACK_LABEL` |
| `playing` | The three items of `HUD_ITEMS` |
| `won` | None |

A control answers only on the screen it belongs to, and the regions on one screen
do not overlap each other, so a point lies in at most one of them. The HUD's
three regions lie inside the HUD strip `specs/table.md` fixes, which holds no
pile, so a press over the table during play is never a press on a control.

## Menu navigation

The controls a screen carries are that screen's menu, in the order the table
above gives them. `menuIndex` is the selected item on that menu, counted from `0`
for the first, and the selected item is drawn distinctly from the others. Every
menu is driven by the keyboard, by a mouse, and by touch alike.

### Keyboard

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The game reads exactly the four actions below, each raised by the keys given
beside it. The runtime layer reads the keyboard by `KeyboardEvent.code`, so the
bindings hold on any layout.
{{else}}
The keyboard belongs to the {{engine.name}} engine. Register exactly the four
actions below, each bound to the keys given beside it. Bindings are
`KeyboardEvent.code` values, so they hold on any layout.
{{/if}}

| Action | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `menu-up` | `ArrowUp`, `KeyW` |
| `menu-down` | `ArrowDown`, `KeyS` |
| `menu-confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` |
| `menu-back` | `Escape` |

Holding a key moves the selection one step rather than repeating it.

| Screen | Action | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` | `menu-up` | `menuIndex` moves up one, wrapping from `0` to the last item. |
| `title` | `menu-down` | `menuIndex` moves down one, wrapping from the last item to `0`. |
| `title` | `menu-confirm` | Activates the item at `menuIndex`, as `specs/screens.md` states. |
| `title` | `menu-back` | Nothing. |
| `howto` | `menu-up`, `menu-down` | Nothing. The screen carries one item, so `menuIndex` stays `0`. |
| `howto` | `menu-confirm`, `menu-back` | Activates the item labelled `HOWTO_BACK_LABEL`, as `specs/screens.md` states. |
| `playing` | `menu-up` | `menuIndex` moves up one over `HUD_ITEMS`, wrapping from `0` to the last item. |
| `playing` | `menu-down` | `menuIndex` moves down one over `HUD_ITEMS`, wrapping from the last item to `0`. |
| `playing` | `menu-confirm` | Activates the HUD item at `menuIndex`, as `specs/screens.md` states. |
| `playing` | `menu-back` | Nothing. |

`won` shows no menu and reads none of the four.

When several edges arrive on one frame, `menu-up` is applied before `menu-down`,
and movement before `menu-confirm`: a frame carrying both an up edge and a down
edge moves up only, and a frame carrying a movement edge and a confirm edge moves
only.

### Pointer and touch

A press is a mouse button going down or a finger touching down and a release is
the button coming up or the finger lifting, as this file's first section states,
so a finger landing and lifting inside one region activates its item exactly as a
mouse click does.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| A mouse moves onto an item's region, its button up or down | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A finger touches down inside an item's region, or travels onto one while down | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A press and the release that follows it both land inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and the item is activated. |
| The two edges of a gesture land in different regions, or either lands outside every region | No item is activated. |

The item every activation acts on is the item at `menuIndex`, whichever input
raised it, and the effect is the one the keyboard table gives `menu-confirm` on
that screen.

## The debug overlay

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The read-only debug overlay `specs/instrumentation.md` describes is shown and
hidden by the backtick key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`. It is off when the
game starts. The overlay and its toggle are part of the runtime layer you write.
{{else}}
The read-only debug overlay is the engine's, shown and hidden by the backtick
key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`, and off when the game starts. What it
shows is in `specs/instrumentation.md`.
{{/if}}
